const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is it realistic to switch from IT to a completely non-IT career in India?',
    a: 'Yes, for specific lanes with real, documented entry routes: teaching and corporate training, HR and L&D, government exams, consulting, entrepreneurship, and non-IT finance roles. It is not realistic to expect an identical salary on day one in most of these lanes. The switch works when you pick one lane, test it before you resign, and accept a defined income reset for a defined period rather than treating the move as a lateral swap.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which non-IT career pays the most for someone coming from IT in India?',
    a: 'Entrepreneurship and specialised consulting have the highest ceiling but the widest range and the most risk in year one. Among stable-income lanes, HR and L&D roles at senior levels and general management consulting positions tend to pay closer to IT-level compensation than teaching, government service, or entry-level roles in a brand-new field. Government roles offer security and a defined pay scale rather than a high ceiling.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much of a pay cut should I expect switching from IT to a non-IT field?',
    a: 'It depends entirely on the lane and your starting seniority. Entry-level government exam roles (SSC CGL, banking PO) commonly start in the Rs 6-11 LPA range regardless of your prior IT salary. Corporate training, L&D, and non-IT consulting roles can land close to your current pay if you can show a portfolio of delivered outcomes. Entrepreneurship and freelancing usually mean near-zero or unpredictable income for the first several months, sometimes longer. Build your decision around a real number, not a hope.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need to quit my IT job before testing a non-IT career?',
    a: 'No, and doing so early is one of the most common expensive mistakes. Most of these lanes can be tested on the side first: teaching a weekend batch, taking one HR-adjacent project, appearing for a government prelims exam while employed, freelancing one client, or shadowing a consultant. Resign only after the test gives you real evidence that the new lane is both wanted by the market and actually enjoyable to you day to day.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is 30 or 35 too late to switch from IT to a non-IT career in India?',
    a: 'No, but the lane and the plan both need to match your stage. Government exams like UPSC have upper age limits (32 for the general category, higher for reserved categories) that genuinely constrain that specific door past a point. Teaching, consulting, HR, entrepreneurship, and non-IT finance roles do not have hard age caps, but they do reward a specific angle — domain depth, a network, or a proof-of-work portfolio — more than a generic "I want to switch" pitch once you are past your early thirties.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which IT skills actually transfer to non-IT careers?',
    a: 'Structured problem-solving, stakeholder communication, documentation discipline, data literacy, and project or timeline management transfer directly into teaching, HR, consulting, and government administrative roles. Coding syntax and specific tech-stack knowledge usually do not transfer directly unless the new role is itself tech-adjacent (edtech content, HR-tech, product analytics). The transferable layer is how you think and communicate under a deadline, not the tools you used to do it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I do an MBA, a certification, or nothing before switching out of IT?',
    a: 'Match the credential to the lane, not the other way round. Teaching a professional or technical subject at degree level usually needs a relevant master\'s degree plus UGC NET, or an ME/M.Tech with 55%+ marks for some engineering-teaching posts. Corporate training and L&D often value a certification (CPLP, ATD, or a specific instructional-design credential) plus a delivered track record more than a fresh degree. Consulting and general management roles are where an MBA helps most, and even there, GMAC\'s own research shows career-pivot intent among MBA applicants falling in recent years, meaning proof and positioning now matter as much as the degree.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the single biggest reason IT-to-non-IT switches fail in India?',
    a: 'Skipping the test phase and betting the whole transition on one big, irreversible move — resigning, then studying full-time for a government exam with no fallback, or launching a business with no runway. The switches that hold up are the ones where someone tested the new lane on the side, got real market feedback, and only then converted the test into a full commitment with a defined financial runway behind it.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://futurecareerschool.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog' },
